<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ise Grand Shrine</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ise Jingu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ise Grand Shrine is a complex of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> shrines at Ise in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mie-prefecture">Mie Prefecture</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, centred on the Inner Shrine of the sun goddess Amaterasu and the Outer Shrine of Toyouke. Its main sanctuaries are rebuilt every twenty years in the same ancient style. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> mass pilgrimages to Ise became one of the great popular movements of Japanese life, bringing trade to the towns along its roads.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ise-grand-shrine.jpg" alt="Ise Grand Shrine" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: N yotarou · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naiku_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
